WEBVTT

00:00.341 --> 00:02.245
- [Voiceover] We're asked
to translate and dilate

00:02.245 --> 00:05.178
the unit circle to map
it onto each circle.

00:05.178 --> 00:07.367
So, this is the unit
circle right over here.

00:07.367 --> 00:09.771
It's centered at zero comma zero.

00:09.771 --> 00:10.990
It has a radius of one.

00:10.990 --> 00:12.673
That's why we call it a unit circle.

00:12.673 --> 00:15.123
When they say translate,
they say move it around.

00:15.349 --> 00:17.685
So, that would be a translation of it.

00:18.269 --> 00:20.366
Dilating it means making it larger.

00:20.366 --> 00:22.978
So, dilating that unit
circle would be doing...

00:22.978 --> 00:24.580
Whoops, I just translated it.

00:24.580 --> 00:26.159
Would be doing something like that.

00:26.159 --> 00:29.328
So, we're gonna translate
and dilate this unit circle

00:29.328 --> 00:30.716
to map it onto each circle.

00:31.059 --> 00:33.491
For example, I could translate
it so that the center

00:33.491 --> 00:36.441
is translated to the center
of that magenta circle,

00:36.441 --> 00:39.668
and I can dilate it so
that it has been mapped

00:39.668 --> 00:41.781
onto that larger magenta circle.

00:41.781 --> 00:43.788
I can do that, let me
do that for a few more.

00:43.788 --> 00:44.760
I'm not gonna do that for all of them

00:44.760 --> 00:47.316
Just to give you the idea of
what they're talking about.

00:47.705 --> 00:50.584
So, now, I'm translating the center of my,

00:50.584 --> 00:52.185
it's no longer a unit circle.

00:52.185 --> 00:53.776
I am translating the center of my circle

00:53.776 --> 00:55.680
to the center of the purple circle.

00:55.680 --> 00:57.225
Now, I'm gonna dilate it

00:57.579 --> 00:59.302
so it has the same radius.

00:59.313 --> 01:00.934
And notice, I can map it.

01:00.934 --> 01:04.416
So, if you can map one shape to another

01:04.416 --> 01:06.234
through translation and dilation,

01:06.234 --> 01:08.056
then the things are, by definition,

01:08.056 --> 01:10.354
they are going to be similar.

01:10.354 --> 01:12.874
This is really just an exercise in seeing

01:12.874 --> 01:14.626
that all circles are similar.

01:14.626 --> 01:16.367
If you just take any circle,

01:16.367 --> 01:19.352
and you make it have the same
center as another circle,

01:19.352 --> 01:23.752
then you can just scale it up or down

01:23.752 --> 01:27.299
to match the circle that you
moved it to the center of.

01:27.594 --> 01:28.488
So, there you have it.

01:28.488 --> 01:29.590
Hopefully, this gives you a sense

01:29.590 --> 01:31.977
that all circles are similar.
